Oh, sure, that goes without saying. There'd need to
be several kinds of tweaks: monster difficulty would
need to become partially turn count or "rooms
explored" based to smoothly increase up to a new,
higher limit per level, you'd need more people food
and pet chow, and comparatively less other stuff
randomly distributed, random and shrieker-evoked
monster generation would need to be focused more
around the PC's location, perhaps in a gaussian
distribution, or the monsters and the PC won't
easily find each other, monsters would need to carry
less, and less useful stuff, flood fill search
mechanisms might need to be rethought, and I'm sure
a dozen more things I haven't thought out very well.

The big item would be devising a way to keep finding
the stairs from becoming mind-deadeningly dull,
though.

I just feel like the existing levels are much too
simplistic as maps to explore (my professional work
in cartography biases me here a lot) and would like
a really good excuse for implementing a level
display system with two resolutions, one local, one
overall, so I can play with the absurd-128 tile set
and not go nuts. I'm told such a display has been
tried, I don't know if it ever got released, though,
and I haven't seen it if it has.

It also offends my inner dungeon explorer that the
levels are rectangular instead of square (or better,
circular) in aspect; that really klunks and stands
out as an artifact left over from earlier screen
size limits and screen aspect ratios.

Bigger levels would also allow enough empty space to
make non-rectangular rooms more doable in randomly
generated levels, to let big rooms be features of
normal levels, like shops are now, and make Mine
Towns capable of being randomly built as well. As
things are now, stuff on special levels has to be
really carefully hand designed and hand fitted to
work.

My months-back comments on adding Larn-flavored
levels, but autogenerated, fit in here somewhere;
they'd be a lot more effective with more room to
grow them, and as special parts of more complex
levels.

Bigger levels would also allow more room for minor
features like trees and barracades that are also too
hard to fit in, and thus not used, now.

